  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Carrie N. Baker’s <em>Abortion Pills</em> is already showing early reach as a pedagogical and policy-relevant book: it has been downloaded 179 times through OAPEN, appears in library holdings, and is being discussed in recorded public talks and author events. The record also places the book in direct conversation with gender equality concerns, while its open-access availability through multiple repository instances should support broad classroom and public use.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>Abortion Pills</em> presents a strong early-impact profile for a recent scholarly book, driven less by citation accumulation than by visible uptake in reading, teaching, and public discussion. The book has 179 recorded downloads, indicating sustained use, and it is available through open repository channels, which likely expands access beyond the publisher site. The title also has library presence, another sign that it is being positioned for consultation and teaching.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest audience signal comes from teaching-adjacent and public-facing use. The record shows four YouTube videos in which the book is discussed, including an author presentation and other recorded conversations focused on abortion pills and reproductive politics. Together, these suggest that the book is being used as an explanatory anchor in public scholarship and educational settings, especially around the history and politics of abortion access.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The topical and societal relevance is also clear. The book’s strongest concept alignment is with abortion and pills, and it maps to the Sustainable Development Goal of gender equality. That combination fits the book’s focus on the history of abortion pills in the United States and its broader relevance to reproductive autonomy, health policy, and gender justice.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the scholarly citation record is still early, with no citations indexed in OpenAlex at the time of this digest. That does not diminish the book’s apparent reach; it simply means the evidence base is currently weighted toward access, teaching, and public engagement rather than formal citation uptake. The overall pattern is typical of a newly released scholarly book whose influence is beginning to form across classrooms, libraries, and public discussion.</p>",
